<Layout title="Practical modelling patterns" description="Reusable patterns for property identities, participation, qualified relationships, part-whole structures, time, measurements, claims and evidence.">
  <h1>Practical modelling patterns</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Good patterns make the same important distinctions consistently. They help us
    model a property, a valuation or a claim without copying a form's layout into
    the ontology or inventing a new mechanism for every working group.
  </p>

  <h2 id="purpose">Choose a pattern by the question it must answer</h2>
  <p>
    Begin with an example and a useful question: who supplied this value, which
    property does it describe, when was it valid, and what supports it? The answer
    determines the information the model must preserve. A pattern earns its place
    by preserving that meaning, not by making a diagram look more sophisticated.
  </p>
  <p>
    The examples below are illustrative design exercises. They are not declarations
    that every named construct exists in the current Property Pack candidate, or
    permission to expand its agreed business scope. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision basis</a> identifies the
    selected ODRs that govern our method and the SPDTF scope decisions. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-rules">modelling rules</a> explain the
    standards and profile boundaries that govern an actual encoding.
  </p>

  <h2 id="thing-description">Separate a thing from descriptions of it</h2>
  <p>
    A building is not its listing, survey report or registry record. Those resources
    describe it for different purposes and may have different authors, dates and
    identifiers. Keeping them distinct lets a model retain conflicting descriptions
    without creating several buildings or treating every description as true.
  </p>
  <p>
    Establish the identity of each thing, then connect descriptions to their subject.
    Ask whether correcting the report creates a new building; whether withdrawing
    the listing removes the property; and whether two records can disagree about
    one subject. Stable identifiers and explicit relationships make these questions
    answerable. Shared text or a coincident address is insufficient identity evidence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="participation">Represent participation in its context</h2>
  <p>
    “Alex is the buyer” is incomplete without the transaction. A participation
    pattern identifies the person or organisation, the activity or relationship,
    and the role played. It can also record a period of participation or the basis
    on which an agent acts for somebody else.
  </p>
  <p>
    That makes simultaneous roles intelligible: the same organisation may provide
    data for one exchange and consume data in another. It also distinguishes
    permission to play a role from actually playing it. A registration or capability
    is not evidence that a particular transaction took place. See
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/identity-roles-and-phases#roles">identity and roles</a>
    for the analysis behind this pattern.
  </p>

  <h2 id="qualified">Qualify a relationship when the relationship carries information</h2>
  <p>
    A direct named connection is often sufficient: a report describes a building.
    Introduce a resource for the association when the association itself needs
    dates, a source, a quantity, a status or evidence. “This party represented this
    client for this matter during this period” is more than a bare connection
    between two parties.
  </p>
  <p>
    Choose the meaning of that resource explicitly: appointment, ownership interest,
    assessment, assertion or participation. A business relationship with its own
    identity may be analysed as a relator; merely adding metadata to a link does
    not make every link one. Conversely, do not scatter relationship-specific facts
    across the participants and lose which association those facts belong to.
  </p>
  <p>
    Qualification is proportionate. A join table in a source database does not by
    itself justify a new ontology class. If the business has nothing to say about
    the association beyond its endpoints, a direct object property may preserve
    the complete meaning.
  </p>

  <h2 id="part-whole">Distinguish parts, members and specialisations</h2>
  <p>
    A roof can be part of a building. A building can belong to a portfolio. A class
    of buildings can specialise another class. These are different relationships:
    physical composition, collection membership and type specialisation. None
    should be inferred from indentation in a document or containment in JSON.
  </p>
  <p>
    Define which parts are meant and whether the whole depends on them. A parcel,
    building and registered title need explicit domain analysis; they are not
    automatically a physical containment tree. State whether a particular
    part-whole relationship is transitive. Transitivity is a property of a defined
    relationship, not a universal consequence of the English words “part of”.
  </p>

  <h2 id="values">Distinguish values, measurements and governed choices</h2>
  <p>
    Use a typed literal for a simple value when its interpretation is unambiguous.
    A measurement may need a unit, measurement method, subject, date and responsible
    assessor. “Area: 85” leaves important questions unanswered: what area, measured
    how, and in which unit? Qualify the value when those distinctions matter to use.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use a governed vocabulary for a named choice whose definition must be shared.
    SKOS supports concepts, labels, alternative labels, definitions and scheme
    membership. The code is not the displayed label. A changed label need not mean
    a changed concept; a changed meaning may need a reviewed version or replacement.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep closed value lists distinct from open reference populations. A delivery
    profile may require one of a finite set of codes, while a reference to an
    organisation may allow any appropriately identified organisation. SHACL can
    express the selected requirement, but the working group must first determine
    which kind of choice the business needs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="time">Keep event time, validity and recording time separate</h2>
  <p>
    An inspection can occur on Monday, be recorded on Tuesday and support a report
    issued on Friday. A fact can concern a period rather than an instant. Those
    dates answer different questions; a single generic “date” cannot preserve them.
    Name the event or interval and record what each timestamp means. Reuse OWL-Time
    for temporal concepts; distinguish the time a state holds from the provenance
    of when its record was created. A state's allowed successor is a transition
    relationship, not a SKOS broader/narrower relationship.
  </p>
  <p>
    Distinguish a later correction from a real-world change. Correcting a misspelt
    name does not necessarily describe a new owner. A new assessment may supersede
    a prior conclusion without making the earlier report disappear. A temporal
    pattern should support the question the consumer needs, including time zone,
    precision and unknown-date handling where relevant.
  </p>

  <h2 id="claims">Keep claims, evidence and provenance connected</h2>
  <p>
    A claim says something about a subject. Evidence is offered in support of that
    claim. Provenance records its source and production history. A report existing
    in a graph does not prove its claims, and a technically valid claim is not
    automatically accurate, current or authoritative for every purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Record who asserted the information, the subject, the relevant time and the
    supporting material where required. Preserve disagreement explicitly: two
    valuations may use different dates, assumptions or purposes. Do not silently
    choose one value merely because its source was loaded last.
  </p>
  <p>
    We reuse PROV-O for origins, attribution and activities. Resource-level
    provenance is sufficient when the resource is an immutable unit with consistent
    provenance. Where statements have different origins or confidence, the selected
    method uses RDF 1.2 statement reification selectively. Keep the annotation
    attached to the exact statement it qualifies rather than maintaining a second
    independent copy. Provenance records evidence about a claim; it does not certify it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="review">Turn a pattern into a reviewable candidate</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the business question and two or three concrete examples.</li>
    <li>Identify the things, choices, relationships and time distinctions involved.</li>
    <li>Assign ownership and identify any meaning that crosses a context boundary.</li>
    <li>Select established vocabulary only where its semantics fit the intended use.</li>
    <li>Define the data constraints and the expected answers to useful queries.</li>
    <li>Review counterexamples with practitioners and record the resulting decision.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Technical checks can expose missing fields, inconsistent representations and
    broken query expectations. Practitioners decide whether the pattern expresses
    the real situation. That division lets contributors participate through
    ordinary examples while giving implementers a precise, reusable model to build.
  </p>
</Layout>
